/**
 * Renders traffic incidents returned by `trafficIncidentDetails()` on the map.
 *
 * Use this module when you fetch incidents through the Incident Details service and want to
 * display them as a developer-controlled overlay. {@link TrafficIncidentsModule} is the
 * vector-tile alternative. It is hidden in the default style, so this module renders on its own
 * without collision. If you've enabled it elsewhere in your app, hide it again before using this
 * module to avoid double-rendering.
 *
 * ## Rendering approach
 *
 * The module draws 7–8 layers: 4 to 5 line layers (optional highlight-halo, outline, solid inner,
 * direction chevron, pattern inner) and 3 symbol layers (incident marker, jam marker with delay
 * text, closed-road marker). Per-magnitude styling uses match expressions on `magnitudeOfDelay`
 * inside a single layer instead of one layer per magnitude. Colours come from the loaded style's
 * incident layers, with built-in defaults when absent.
 * They are read again whenever a `traffic.incidents.colors.*` styling knob recolours them.
 * Direction renders as a tiled `line-pattern` (a chevron sprite registered at init) instead of the
 * vector-tile module's per-feature `roads-arrow` symbol. The pattern is continuous, so it reads on
 * short jams and at planning zooms (z12+), while the per-feature symbol only activates at z15+.
 *
 * ## Focus and highlight
 *
 * {@link setFocus} writes MapLibre feature-state `focused=true|false` on each rendered incident.
 * The default highlight widens the focused subset and paints an outline beneath it (black on
 * light map styles, white on dark). A hovered or clicked incident (its `eventState`, see
 * {@link setEventState}) draws the same highlight. Unfocused incidents stay as they are unless you
 * set `highlight.unfocusedOpacityFactor`, which fades them while a focus is active. To override or
 * disable the highlight, use the `highlight` config. With `highlight: false`, `setFocus` still
 * writes feature-state but nothing draws it, so you can drive your own styling off
 * `feature-state.focused` and `eventState`.
 *
 * ## API limitations
 *
 * The Incident Details REST API returns a feature list, not a rendering-ready tile.
 * Several tags that the vector-tile pipeline injects per-feature are absent:
 *
 *  - Road classification (`road_category`, `road_subcategory`). The vector-tile pipeline scales
 *    line width and offset by road class (motorway wider than street, stripe placed alongside the
 *    road on one carriageway). Without it, per-road-class geometry becomes a guess, so the module
 *    renders every incident as a uniform stripe centred on the road, with no offset.
 *  - Traffic-direction side (`left_hand_traffic`). It only matters with road-class-driven offset,
 *    so it is irrelevant here.
 *  - Declutter priority (`display_class`). The vector-tile pipeline hides low-priority incidents
 *    at low zooms. The module shows everything above its layer's minzoom, and the icons of
 *    shorter delays only from a higher zoom.
 *  - Endpoint restriction (`point_type`). The vector-tile pipeline limits jam and closed-road
 *    icons to line endpoints. The module renders them on every matching feature.
 *  - Secondary cause (`icon_category_1`). The vector-tile pipeline layers a cause icon (e.g.
 *    accident) over the jam icon. The module shows only the primary category.
 *
 * For full per-road-class visual fidelity, use {@link TrafficIncidentsModule} instead.
 *
 * ## Known limitations
 *
 * - Overlapping incidents. When multiple incidents share the same point or stack along the same
 *   road segment, MapLibre's symbol collision culling keeps only the one with the longest delay.
 *   Culled features are also invisible to `queryRenderedFeatures`, so hover and click cannot
 *   reach them. To surface multiple incidents at one location, query the source data directly
 *   (the `FeatureCollection` you passed to {@link show}) rather than relying on render-time
 *   hit-testing.
 *
 * @example
 * Fetch and render incidents for Amsterdam:
 * ```typescript
 * import { TomTomConfig } from '@tomtom-org/maps-sdk/core';
 * import { TomTomMap, TrafficIncidentDetailsModule } from '@tomtom-org/maps-sdk/map';
 * import { trafficIncidentDetails } from '@tomtom-org/maps-sdk/services';
 *
 * TomTomConfig.instance.put({ apiKey: '<API_KEY>' });
 *
 * const map = new TomTomMap({ mapLibre: { container: 'map', center: [4.9, 52.37], zoom: 13 } });
 *
 * const incidentDetailsModule = await TrafficIncidentDetailsModule.create(map);
 * const result = await trafficIncidentDetails({
 *     bbox: [4.85, 52.34, 4.95, 52.40],
 *     timeValidityFilter: ['present'],
 * });
 * await incidentDetailsModule.show(result);
 *
 * // Highlight a subset.
 * incidentDetailsModule.setFocus([result.features[0].properties.id]);
 * ```
 *
 * @see {@link TrafficIncidentsModule} — the vector-tile alternative. Hidden in the
 *   default style; if you've enabled it explicitly elsewhere in your app, hide it again
 *   before using this module to avoid double-rendering.
 * @see [Traffic Incident Details Guide](https://docs.tomtom.com/maps-sdk-js/guides/map/traffic-incident-details)
 * @see [Traffic Incident Details Service](https://docs.tomtom.com/maps-sdk-js/guides/services/traffic/traffic-incident-details)
 *
 * @group Traffic Incident Details
 */
